# Product — Mench (Ludo)

## Game rules (v1)

**Standard international Ludo only** — no Persian Mench variants (no blockades, no stacked pieces). See `decisions.mdc` O9, O12.

| Topic | Rule |
| ----- | ---- |
| Players | **2–4** active seats (unused colors disabled) |
| Pieces | 4 per player |
| Dice | One six-sided die per roll |
| Enter board | Roll **6** to move a piece from yard onto starting square |
| Movement | Move one piece forward by die value; choose among legal moves |
| Extra turn | Rolling **6** grants another roll (after moving if a legal move exists) |
| Three sixes | Third **6** in a row: turn ends, no move on that third roll |
| Captures | Landing on opponent (not on safe square) sends them home |
| Safe squares | Star/marked squares — no capture |
| Home column | Color-specific path; must enter with exact count |
| Finish | Exact die count to place on home triangle; all four home = win |
| Blockades | **Off** — at most one piece per square |

## New game setup

All composition is chosen in **New game** preferences before play starts:

| Setting | Options |
| ------- | ------- |
| Player count | 2, 3, or 4 |
| Per seat | **Human** or **CPU** |
| Mode | Local (hotseat) or Online (host configures; joiners take human seats) |

- **0–4 humans** allowed — e.g. all-human (4), solo vs 3 CPU (1+3), all-CPU demo (0 humans, if offered), or any mix.
- At least **one human** required to start a normal game (skip or hide all-CPU unless useful for testing).
- Online: humans claim seats when joining; unclaimed seats run as **CPU** if the host enabled CPU fill for empty seats.

## Turn timer

- **30 seconds** per human turn (roll and/or move combined in one turn window, reset when turn passes).
- Visible countdown; at **0** the turn is forfeited and passes to the next player (no roll, or no move if already rolled — implement as skip turn per rules engine).
- Applies to **local and online** human seats. CPUs are not timed.
- Audio/haptic optional later; v1 = visible bar or numeric countdown only.

## CPU behavior

- CPUs **try to win**: prioritize entering pieces, captures, blocking threats, advancing toward home, and using sixes well.
- Think time: short delay (≈300–800ms) so moves feel considered, not instant.
- No trash-talk or fake “personality” in v1.

## Visual design (branding)

- **Classic Mench / Ludo look:** four familiar player colors (red, green, yellow, blue), cross-shaped board, yards in corners, home triangles — recognizable at a glance.
- **Minimal chrome:** thin borders, flat fills, little ornament; let the board and pieces carry the identity.
- No custom logo or marketing kit yet — use simple wordmark “Mench” and placeholder PWA icons until art is added.
- Tokens live in `src/shared/styles/tokens.css` (phase 2.1).

## UX targets

- **Minimal chrome:** board, dice, current player, setup, timer, win overlay.
- **Animations:** dice roll (tumble/settle), piece slide along path, capture knock-home, subtle turn highlight.
- **Touch-first:** large tap targets for dice and movable pieces; works in portrait on phones.
- **Feedback:** illegal moves rejected in-place; legal moves highlighted; capture and extra-turn called out briefly.

## Online multiplayer (v1)

- Host starts room → **shareable code/link** (no public lobby).
- Joiners enter code or open link; optional display name.
- Server is source of truth; reconnect within session TTL if disconnected briefly.
- **30s turn timer** enforced server-side for human seats.
- If host leaves, promote another connected human or end game (phase 4.3).

## PWA

- Installable from browser (manifest + icons).
- `standalone` display; safe areas for notched phones.
- Offline: cached shell only; online play requires network.

## Non-goals (v1)

- Accounts, friends lists, rankings, chat
- Monetization, ads, cosmetics shop
- Persian Mench variant rules
- Match replays / history
- Voice/video
- Tournament brackets
